WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.639
We'll spend the next few minutes going over what happens after an escalation fires.

2
00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:11.279
The handoff payload is what determines whether the next actor can actually resolve the situation

3
00:00:11.279 --> 00:00:15.000
or has to reconstruct the entire conversation from scratch.

4
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.959
We'll cover the four fields a payload should have, why structure beats narrative, how routing

5
00:00:20.959 --> 00:00:25.719
works, and how to close the loop when a resolution comes back.

6
00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:29.520
An escalation is only as good as the bundle it hands forward.

7
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:35.439
That bundle is the handoff payload, the structured package of context, history, and suggested

8
00:00:35.439 --> 00:00:39.840
next step that an agent passes on when it escalates.

9
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:43.080
The word structured is doing real work there.

10
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.840
A free text block and a schema with defined fields are very different things, and they

11
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.159
produce very different outcomes for whoever receives them.

12
00:00:53.159 --> 00:00:58.639
Escalation routing is how that payload gets directed to the right next actor based on

13
00:00:58.799 --> 00:01:00.119
what it contains.

14
00:01:00.119 --> 00:01:06.720
And closing the loop is the return trip, re-injecting the reviewer's or supervisor's response back

15
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.599
into the original agent's context once the escalation resolves.

16
00:01:11.599 --> 00:01:16.959
Without that last step, an escalation is a one-way street and the agent never learns

17
00:01:16.959 --> 00:01:19.040
what was decided.

18
00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:21.519
A handoff payload needs four things.

19
00:01:21.519 --> 00:01:27.519
First, a conversation summary, a compressed account of what happened before the escalation fired.

20
00:01:27.519 --> 00:01:29.800
Second, the triggering question.

21
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.400
Preserve it verbatim.

22
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:35.680
Don't summarize it and don't paraphrase it because the reviewer needs to see exactly

23
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.879
what the agent saw at the moment the trigger fired.

24
00:01:38.879 --> 00:01:43.519
Third, what the agent tried, and that's a log rather than a summary.

25
00:01:43.519 --> 00:01:49.440
Each tool call made, each validation run, each retry attempted, along with what it returned

26
00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:55.000
because that log is how the reviewer knows what not to bother trying again.

27
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.879
And fourth, a suggested next step.

28
00:01:57.879 --> 00:02:02.080
Agents often skip this one since they escalated precisely because they didn't know what to

29
00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:08.399
do, but even an uncertain hypothesis gives the reviewer somewhere to start.

30
00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:12.559
Structured handoffs and narrative dumps aren't equally useful, and the difference shows up

31
00:02:12.559 --> 00:02:15.880
immediately when you try to route or aggregate them.

32
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.039
A structured handoff has defined fields.

33
00:02:19.039 --> 00:02:23.759
The receiver can parse it, route it to the right queue, and extract the fields they need

34
00:02:23.759 --> 00:02:25.759
without reading everything.

35
00:02:25.759 --> 00:02:28.639
An automated system can do that routing too.

36
00:02:28.639 --> 00:02:31.119
A narrative dump is a free text block.

37
00:02:31.119 --> 00:02:36.300
It might contain everything, or it might omit the one detail the reviewer needed most.

38
00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:43.000
It can't be parsed reliably, can't be routed automatically, and can't be aggregated into metrics.

39
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.279
When you're looking at the data across 100 escalations, free text is nearly useless.

40
00:02:48.279 --> 00:02:53.960
Structure wins because it's designed for the receiver, not for the agent that produced it.

41
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.720
Once a payload is structured, routing becomes straightforward.

42
00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.960
The orchestrator inspects the payload and directs it to one of three destinations.

43
00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:08.520
Human reviewer is the right destination when the escalation requires judgment or authority

44
00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.600
that a model shouldn't exercise.

45
00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:16.919
A specialist agent is the right destination when the issue falls within another agent's defined domain.

46
00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:22.279
A supervisor model is the right destination when the escalating agent hit its capability

47
00:03:22.279 --> 00:03:26.320
limit, but a more capable model might be able to proceed.

48
00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.539
The routing logic lives in the orchestrator, not in the escalating agent.

49
00:03:30.539 --> 00:03:33.520
The agent's job is to produce a clean payload.

50
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.679
The orchestrator's job is to send it somewhere.

51
00:03:36.679 --> 00:03:43.720
Keeping those responsibilities separate makes both components easier to test and easier to change.

52
00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:47.759
Three failure patterns show up repeatedly in handoff designs.

53
00:03:47.759 --> 00:03:49.960
Missing context is the most common.

54
00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:55.039
The payload describes what failed, but omits enough of the conversation history that the

55
00:03:55.039 --> 00:03:58.679
reviewer has to go back and read the whole thing anyway.

56
00:03:58.679 --> 00:04:01.559
That defeats the purpose of a structured handoff.

57
00:04:01.559 --> 00:04:04.360
The second failure is no suggested step.

58
00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.800
The agent describes the problem, but offers nothing on what to do next.

59
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.619
The reviewer is left to figure that out alone.

60
00:04:11.619 --> 00:04:14.240
The third failure is an unroutable format.

61
00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:19.119
A free text payload can't be parsed by the routing layer, so it lands in a generic queue

62
00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.320
instead of the right inbox.

63
00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.880
All three failures come from designing the payload for the agent's convenience, rather

64
00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.559
than the receiver's needs.

65
00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:30.600
Every escalation is a data point.

66
00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.720
An agent that escalates repeatedly on the same trigger category is telling you something

67
00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.679
about your prompt, your policy, or your threshold settings.

68
00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:45.160
The only way to see that pattern is to log each handoff and aggregate over time.

69
00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:51.519
Log the trigger type, the payload fields, the routing destination, and the resolution.

70
00:04:51.519 --> 00:04:55.839
Aggregate that data across sessions, and you'll find patterns quickly.

71
00:04:55.839 --> 00:05:00.480
Maybe one trigger category is responsible for 60% of your escalations.

72
00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:05.480
That probably means the threshold is set too low, or there's an allow list gap.

73
00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:10.480
Maybe escalations to the supervisor model almost always resolve successfully, which

74
00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.799
suggests the issue is capability, not policy.

75
00:05:14.799 --> 00:05:20.640
Routing turns individual escalations into systemic improvements to your agent design.

76
00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:24.640
Closing the loop is the step that most agent designs omit.

77
00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.899
After a human reviewer or supervisor model resolves the escalation, that resolution needs

78
00:05:29.899 --> 00:05:32.079
to come back to the original agent.

79
00:05:32.079 --> 00:05:36.920
The injection point is the same context window the agent escalated from.

80
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:42.040
The reviewer's answer gets appended, and the agent resumes with that new information.

81
00:05:42.040 --> 00:05:46.320
When we talk about loop closure, the agent restarts from its pre-escalation state.

82
00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.500
It doesn't know what the reviewer decided.

83
00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:53.559
It might escalate again on the same trigger, or it might proceed incorrectly because it

84
00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:56.000
never received the resolution.

85
00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.619
With loop closure, escalation becomes a recovery mechanism.

86
00:05:59.619 --> 00:06:03.399
The agent hits a limit, gets help, and continues.

87
00:06:03.399 --> 00:06:10.200
That's the design goal for any agent that needs to handle complex multi-step tasks reliably.

88
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:17.040
A well-structured handoff payload gives the next actor everything they need to act without starting over.

89
00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:22.140
A defined set of fields instead of a narrative dump, clear routing to the destination that

90
00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:29.440
can actually resolve the issue, and loop closure at the end are what turn an escalation into

91
00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:33.040
a recovery mechanism, rather than a dead end.

92
00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:37.399
Build the payload for the receiver, not for the agent that produced it.

93
00:06:37.399 --> 00:06:38.760
That wraps up this lesson.

94
00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:39.920
I'll see you in the next one.

